- 8 types of Sudation: Sankara, Prastara, Seka, Nadi, Drona, Jala, Udakoshta, Kuti.
- 8 divisions, 120 chapters.
- Fever in animals:
  - Cows, dogs – Charana
  - Matsya – Indrajala
  - Sakuna – Pramilaka
  - Sarva Dhanya – Chitraka
  - Kanda moola, Phala – Dava
  - Hasti – Phalana
  - Aswa – Utkarna
  - Sarvamaya – Tejas
  - Others – Artaka(va)
- Regional diseases:
  - Matsya + Anna – Kaphra Prakopa, Slipada, Galaganda
  - Dakshina Desa – Kushta
  - Kamboja Desa – Arshas
  - Paschima Desa – Rajayakshma
  - Bahlika Desa – Balasaka

### 5.3 Jatukarna

- One of six disciples of Atreya; contemporary of Agnivesa, Bhela.
- Wrote Jatukarna Samhita / Jatukarna Tantra; not available.
- Quoted by Jejjata, Chakrapani, Dalhana, Arunadatta, Vijayarakshita, Srikanthadatta, Shivadas Sen.
- Literally “bat-eared.”
- In Satapatha Brahmana, propounder of Brahma Vidya.

### 5.4 Parasara

- One of six disciples of Atreya; contemporary of Agnivesa.
- Wrote Parasara Samhita; not available.
- In Hasti-Ayurveda, Rishis Achuda, Matanga, Urmimali invited by Romapada to learn Hasti Ayurveda under Parasara.
- Another work: Takra-Kalpa.
- Quoted by Vagbhata, Dalhana, Chakrapani, Hemadri, Shivadas Sen.
- Opinion: umbilicus first organ formed in fetus; Siras start from Guda sthana.
- Popular up to 15th century AD.

### 5.5 Harita

- One of six disciples of Atreya.
- Composed Harita Samhita; original not available.
- Another Harita: disciple of Jabali, author of Dharma Sastra and Smriti.
- Quoted in Tatwachandrika, Tatwapradeepika, Bhavaprakasa, Chikitsakalika, Charaka, Susruta, Shivadas Sen.
- Harita Samhita published from Calcutta, 1887; language suggests later period.

### 5.6 Ksharapani

- One of six disciples of Atreya; colleague of Agnivesa, Bhela, Jatukarna.
- Composed Ksharapani Samhita; not available.
- Quoted by Susruta, Vijayarakshita, Shivadas Sen.
- Prognostic aspects of Sodha quoted in Madhukosa.
- Features of Balasa Jwara quoted by Susruta.
- Vagbhata quoted in Rakta Gulma.
- Shivadas Sen took Pippalyadi Leha for Pitta Kshayaja Kasa.

### 5.7 Susruta

- Disciple of Divodasa Dhanvantari, King of Kasi.
- Salya Tantra top priority.
- Dhanvantari = master of surgery; tradition Dhanvantareya.
- Son of Viswamitra (according to Ramayana).
- Bhavaprakasa: Viswamitra sent Susruta to Divodasa Dhanvantari.
- Date: between 1500–1000 BC.
- Royal physicians should be well versed in theory and practice.
- Dissection of dead body by Acharya Susruta; every medical student should conduct dissection.
- Surgical techniques practiced on flowers, fruits, leather bags, dead animals.
- Rhinoplasty: Maratha Vaidya reconstructed nose of Kawasaji; published in Gentleman’s Magazine, 1794; J.C. Carque performed similar operations 1814; Indian Plastic Surgery.
- Susruta Samhita: 120 chapters + Uttara Tantra 66.
- Details: Sutra Sthana 46, Nidana 16, Sareera 10, Chikitsa 40, Kalpa 8, Uttara 66.
- Topics: fundamental principles, surgical instruments, Kshara, Agni, Jalouka, cauterization, medicinal herbs, emetics, purgatives, etiology, pathology, embryology, anatomy, Panchakarma, toxicology, etc.
- Humoral theory, sleep, dreams, time, fetal development, blood circulation, urine formation, diseases, Madhumeha, poisons.
- Surgical procedures: pre-operative, operative, post-operative; practical training.
- Grafting, fractures, dislocations, piles, fistula, cataract, tooth extraction.
- Training and duties of physicians, surgeons, nurses.

### 5.8 Aupadhenava and Other Disciples

- Aupadhenava, Vaitarana, Aurabhra, Poushkavalvata, Karavriya, Gopura Rakshita: colleagues of Susruta; disciples of Dhanvantari.
- Each wrote treatises; not available.
- Bhoja: quoted in Susruta Samhita; views on Alkalies, diet for Vranita, Grahaniroga, Rajayakshma, Murcha, Vishpota, Mukharoga.
- Karavinya Tantra: Salya Tantra; quoted in Madhukosa.
- Gopurarakshita: quoted in Tatwachandrka.
- Vaitarana: specialist in surgery; quoted by Dalhana.
- Bhaluki Tantra: quoted by Chakrapanidutta, Dalhana, Vijayarakshita, Srikanthadatta.
- Videha Tantra / Nimi Tantra: eye diseases; quoted by Dalhana, Vijayarakshita, Srikanthadatta.
- Kankayana Tantra, Gargya Tantra, Galava Tantra, Satyaki Tantra, Sounaka Tantra, Karala Tantra, Krishnatreya Tantra, Chakshusya Tantra.

### 5.9 Jivaka

- Eminent physician of Buddha’s time, 6th century BC.
- Born to Salavati, call girl of Vaisali; brought up by prince Abhaya; called Kumara Bachcha.
- Learned Ayurveda from Atreya at Takshasila for 7 years.
- Asked to bring plant not used in medicine; returned empty-handed.
- Practiced at Rajagaha.
- Cured merchant’s wife’s headache with Nasya Karma.
- Cured Bimbisara’s fistula-in-ano.
- Cranial operation on merchant; pulled out two worms.
- Laparotomy on merchant’s son.
- Treated King Pradyota of Ujjain for Jaundice.
- Treated Buddha for constipation with medicated lotus flower (Aghrana Yoga).
- Treated Bouddha Bhikshus for Ajirna.
- Traveled to Egypt; performed cranial operation.
- Neurosurgery: cutting nerve at root.
- Possessed herb Bhaishajya Raja illuminating internal organs.

### 5.10 Nimi

- First to compose treatise on Salakya Tantra: Nimi Tantra / Videha Tantra.
- Susruta took references.
- King Nimi and Vaideha same.
- Bhagavata: Nimi, son of Ikshwaku, conducted Yagna without Vasishta; cursed; body preserved; churned; boy born -> Janak, Vaideha, Midhila.

