## Chapter 6: Redactors (Pratisamskarthas)

### 6.1 Charaka

- Redacted Agnivesa Tantra -> Charaka Samhita.
- Court physician of Kanishka, 2nd century AD (Chinese translation of Samyukta Ratna Pitahaka Sutra).
- Vedic times: branch of Krishna Yajurveda known as Charaka; Vaisampayana had disciple Charaka.
- Meaning: wandering physician.
- Some identify with Patanjali; not true.
- Bhavaprakasa: Charaka = incarnation of Sesa.
- Date: 3rd–2nd century BC based on external and internal evidence.
- 43 Sanskrit commentaries.
- Translated into Arabic in 8th century AD.
- Charaka’s Club established in New York, 1898.

### 6.2 Dridhabala

- Redactor of Charaka Samhita.
- Son of Kapilabala; native of Panchanadapura, Kashmir (Panjhor).
- 4th century AD.
- Redacted 17 chapters Chikitsa, 12 Kalpa, 12 Siddhi.
- Redactor: elaborates concise, concises elaborate, modernizes ancient work.

### 6.3 Nagarjuna

- Believed to have redacted Susruta Samhita and supplemented Uttara Tantra.
- More than 14 references; ~100 works attributed.
- Contemporary of Kanishka; author of Upaya Hridaya.
- Bodhisattva 13th; chief disciple Arya Deva 14th.
- Madhyamika school; lived 150–250 CE in South India.
- Nagarjuna konda in Guntur, Andhra Pradesh.
- Works: Madhyandina Karika, Ratnavali, Vigraha Vyavartini, Suhrullekha, Dwadasa Mukha Sastra, Maha Prajna paramita Sastra.
- Alchemist Nagarjuna: 8th–9th century AD; Rasendra Mangala, Kakshaputa Tantra, Yogaratnamala.
- Al-Beruni: Nagarjuna great scholar of Alchemy, 9th–10th AD.
- Redactor of Susruta Samhita: 4th–5th century AD.

### 6.4 Vatsya

- Kasyapa Samhita redacted by Vatsya.
- Kasyapa Samhita lost in Kali Yuga; conserved by Yaksha Anayasa; Vatsya obtained and redacted in eight parts.

