### 7.1 Overview

- Gupta period: golden period; Nalanda University established during Kumara Gupta I (414–445 AD).
- Need for concise texts; Vagbhata composed Ashtanga Sangraha.
- Sangraha period: 5th–15th century AD.
- Works:
  - Tisata – Chikitsakalika – 5th cent. AD
  - Chandrata – Commentary on Chikitsakalika – 6th cent. AD
  - Madhavakara – Rigvinishchaya / Madhavanidana – 7th cent. AD
  - Vrinda – Siddhayoga – 9th cent. AD
  - Chakrapani – Chakradutta – 11th cent. AD
  - Sarangadhara – Sarangadhara Samhita – 13th cent. AD
  - Bhavamishra – Bhavaprakasa – 16th cent. AD
  - Yogaratnakara – 17th–18th cent. AD
  - Govindadas – Bhaishajya Ratnavali – 18th cent. AD

### 7.2 Charaka Samhita

- Brihat Trayee: Charaka Samhita, Susruta Samhita, Ashtanga Sangraha/Hridaya.
- Charaka Samhita: general medicine.
- 8 sections, 120 chapters:
  - Sutrasthana – 30 chapters
  - Nidana Sthana – 8 chapters
  - Vimana Sthana – 8 chapters
  - Indriya Sthana – 12 chapters
  - Chikitsa Sthana – 30 chapters
  - Kalpa Sthana – 12 chapters
  - Siddhi Sthana – 12 chapters
- Sutrasthana subdivided into Sapta Chatushkas:
  1. Bheshaja Chatushka
  2. Swasta Chatushka
  3. Nirdesa Chatushka
  4. Kalpana Chatushka
  5. Roga Chatushka
  6. Yojana Chatushka
  7. Annapana Chatushka
- Four types of Sutras: Guru Sutra, Sishya Sutra, Pratisasmkarta Sutra, Ekiya Sutra.
- Commentaries:
  1. Charakanyasa – Bhattara Harischandra – 4th cent. AD
  2. Charaka Panjika – Swami Kumara – after 4th cent. AD
  3. Nirantarapada Vyakhya – Jejjata – 6th cent. AD
  4. Ayurveda Dipika – Chakrapani – 11th cent. AD
  5. Tatwa Chandrika – Shivadas Sen – 15th cent. AD
  6. Jalpakalpataru – Gangadhar Sen – 19th cent. AD
  7. Charakopaskara – Yogendranath Sen – 20th cent. AD
  8. Charaka Pradipika – Jyotishchandra Saraswati – 20th cent. AD

### 7.3 Susruta Samhita

- Original: 120 chapters, 5 divisions:
  - Sutra Sthana – 46 chapters
  - Nidana Sthana – 16 chapters
  - Sareera Sthana – 10 chapters
  - Chikitsa Sthana – 40 chapters
  - Kalpa Sthana – 8 chapters
- Uttara Tantra – 66 chapters; addition by Nagarjuna.
- Contents:
  - Sutra: fundamental principles, surgical instruments, Kshara, Agni, Jalouka, cauterization, medicinal herbs, emetics, purgatives.
  - Nidana: etiology, signs, symptoms, pathogenesis.
  - Sareera: embryology, anatomy.
  - Chikitsa: treatment, Panchakarma.
  - Kalpa: toxicology.
  - Uttara: other subjects.
- Theories: cosmic evolution, embryonic conception, humoral theory, sleep, dreams, time, fetal development, blood circulation, urine formation, diseases, Madhumeha, poisons.
- Surgical procedures: pre-operative, operative, post-operative; practical training.
- Grafting, fractures, piles, fistula, cataract, tooth extraction.
- Commentators:
  1. Jejjata – N.A.
  2. Gayadas – Nyaya Chandrika
  3. Brahma Deva – Tippani
  4. Bhaskaracharya – Panjika
  5. Chakrapani – Bhanumati
  6. Dalhana – Nibandha Sangraha
  7. Harana Chandra Chakravarti – Susrutardha Sandipani

### 7.4 Vagbhata

- Son of Simhagupta; grandson of Vagbhata.
- Region of river Sindhu.
- Disciple of Avalokita, chief monk of Mahayana Buddhism.
- Author of Ashtanga Sangraha and Ashtanga Hridaya.
- Date: 4th–5th century AD.
- Ashtanga Sangraha: 6 divisions, 150 chapters:
  - Sutra Sthana – 40
  - Sareera Sthana – 12
  - Nidana Sthana – 16
  - Chikitsa Sthana – 24
  - Kalpa Sthana – 8
  - Uttara Tantra – 50
- Ashtanga Hridaya: 6 sections, 120 chapters:
  - Sutra Sthana – 30
  - Sharira Sthana – 6
  - Nidana Sthana – 16
  - Chikitsa Sthana – 22
  - Kalpa Sthana – 6
  - Uttara Tantra – 40
- Commentaries:
  - Sasilekha – Indu
  - Sarvanga Sundari – Arunadatta
  - Ayurveda Rasayana – Hemadri
  - Padardha Chandrika – Chandranandana
  - Hridaya Bodhika – Sridasa Pandita
  - Tatwa Bodha – Shivadas Sen

### 7.5 Madhavakara

- 7th century AD; son of Indukara; Vanga Desa.
- Author of Rigvinishchaya / Madhavanidana.
- Compilation from Charaka, Susruta, Vagbhata, Ravigupta’s Suddhasara.
- 79 diseases described.
- New diseases: Amavata, Parinamasoola, Annadravasula, Medoroga, Sitapitta, Almapitta, Masurika, Pakshmasata, Yonikanda.
- Commentaries: Madhukosa by Vijayarakhsita & Srikanthadatta; Atanakadarpana by Vachaspati.

### 7.6 Sarangadhara

- 13th–14th AD.
- Sarangadhara Samhita; Laghu Trayee.
- 3 divisions, 32 chapters, 2,600 verses:
  - Poorva Khanda – 7 chapters
  - Madhyama Khanda – 12 chapters
  - Uttara Khanda – 13 chapters
- Topics: formulations, pulse examination, food processing, Pancha Vidha Kashaya Kalpanas, Panchakarma, hygiene, ophthalmic treatments.
- Commentaries: Dipika by Adhamalla, Gudardha Dipika by Kasiram Vaidya, Ayurveda Dipika by Rudra Bhatt, commentary by Vopadeva.

### 7.7 Bhavamishra

- 16th century AD; father Latakamishra; Magadha.
- Bhavaprakasa: 10,268 verses; 3 sections, 7 parts, 80 chapters.
  - Poorva Khanda – 7 chapters
  - Madhyama Khanda – 71 chapters
  - Uttara Khanda – 2 chapters
- First to describe Phiranga Roga; Rasa Karpura; Madhusnuhi.
- Works: Bhavaprakasa, Guna Ratnamala, Madhava Nidana Tippani, Vaidya Nighantu.

### 7.8 Vrinda Madhava

- 9th century AD; Bengal.
- Siddhayoga / Vrinda Madhava.
- Followed Madhavakara’s Rigvinishchaya.
- Herbal formulations only; no metals/minerals.
- Commentary: Vyakhyana Kusumavali by Srikanthadatta, revised by Narayana.

### 7.9 Tisata

- Son of Vagbhata; 10th century AD.
- Chikitsakalika: 400 verses; 25% basic principles, 50% general medicine, 25% other branches.
- Chandrata, son of Tisata, wrote commentary.

### 7.10 Govindadas

- 18th century AD; Bengal.
- Bhaishajyaratnavali; specialized textbook on medicine.
- Similar to Yogaratnakara and Madhavanidana.
- Revised by Bhishagratna Brahma Sankar, 1893; Hindi commentary Vidyodhini by Kaviraj Ambikadatta Shastry.

### 7.11 Yogaratnakara

- 16th century AD? Author unknown.
- 3 parts: Poorva Khanda, Madhyama Khanda, Uttara Khanda.
- New drugs: Satapatra, Tambaku, Kshupaja Maricha, Sevaphala, Ahiphena, Parasika Yavani, Kabab, Khus Khus.
- Translated into Telugu by Yeturi Srinivasacharyulu, 1939.
- Hindi commentary Vaidya Prabha by Dr. Indradev Tripathi and Dr. Dayashankar Tripathi, 1998.

### 7.12 Basawarajeeyam

- 16th century AD; Kotthuru Basava Raju; Andhra Desa.
- Referred to Charaka, Susruta, Madhava, Vagbhata, Bhairava Kalpa, Rasarnava, etc.
- Use of Ahiphena, Rasakarpura, mineral preparations.
- Popular in Andhra like Bhaishajya Ratnavali in Bengal, Yogaratnakara in Maharashtra, Sarangadhara Samhita in Gujarat.

### 7.13 Chakradutta

- Chakrapanidutta; 11th century AD.
- Chikitsa Sangraha / Chakradutta.
- Historical landmark; first representative work of medieval era.
- Followed Vrinda’s Siddhayoga; added mercurial preparations.
- Rasa Parpati first described.
- Kshara Sutra for sinus and fistula-in-ano.

### 7.14 Rajamartanda

- King Bhoja; 11th century AD.
- Also known as Yogasarasangraha or Nanavidhayogasangraha.
- 34 chapters; disease-oriented classification.
- Indication of Asthisamharika in Asthibhagna and Vataroga first reported.
- 250 herbs, 20 minerals, 48 animal substances.
- English translation by Dr. K. Nishteswar & Dr. R. Vidyanath, 2007.

### 7.15 Sahasrayogam

- Originally Sanskrit; translated into Malayalam.
- Practical prescriber in Kerala; home remedies.
- 700–1200 formulations.
- Kalpanas: Kashaya, Churna, Ghrita, Taila, Vati, Gutika, Asava, Arishta, Lehyas.
- Study after Ashtanga Hridaya in Kerala.
- English translation by Nishteswar & Vidyanath, 2006.

