## Chapter 9: Origin and Development of Rasasastra

### 9.1 Meaning and Early History

- Rasa meanings:
  1. Rasa Dhatu
  2. Rasa-Swarasa
  3. Rasa-Nava Rasa
  4. Rasam food
  5. Parada/mercury
- Rasasastra: science of mercury and metallic substances in medicine.
- Indus Valley: gold, silver, copper, tin, lead, bronze; Shilajatu.
- Vedic period: minerals in Rigveda and Adharvana Veda.
- Upanishads and Brahmanas: gold, iron, lead, silver.
- Adharvana Veda: lead used to kill parasites.
- Koutilya’s Ardhasastra: Parada and Hingula; mercury for gold from base metals.
- Kushans: trade with Rome, Farsi; gold imported, gems exported, copper exported.
- Mahabharata: regions Parada, Darad.
- Samhitas: limited mercury references; herbal drugs stressed.
- Charaka: Loha, Mandura, Tuttha; Sthavara, Jangama, Pardhiva.
- Susruta: Anjana application; surgical instruments from metals.
- Ashtanga Sangraha: metals in Rasa, Guna, Virya, Vipaka, Prabhava.
- 6th century AD: Rasa Siddhas demonstrated miracles.
- 7th century AD: Bana’s Harsha Charitra – internal mercury unknown.
- 8th–10th century AD: Sodhana and Marana known.
- Nagarjuna: 8th–9th century AD; one of 84 Rasa Siddhas; propounder of Rasasastra.
- Rasayana therapies: Kuti Pravesika, Vatatapika; mercurial preparations easier.

### 9.2 Works on Rasasastra

1. Rasa Hridaya – Govinda Bhagavat Padacharya
2. Rasendra Mangala
3. Kakshaputa Tantra
4. Rasa Ratnakara – Nagarjuna
5. Rasarnava – 12th century AD
6. Raseswara Darshana – Sayana Madhavacharya

### 9.3 Importance and Specialty

- Aims: reduce aging, healthy, happy, long life.
- Two traditions: Brahmi Sampradaya (Brahma), Saiyi Sampradaya (Siva/Rudra).
- Saiyi Sampradaya: Daiyi and Asuri.
- Rasa Siddhas: Puja to Rasa Linga; mercury = Virya of Siva; sulphur = Rajas of Parvathi.
- Advantages:
  - No need for Doshas, Dushyas, Desa, Kala.
  - Minute dosage.
  - Deep penetration, quick relief.
  - Not dependent on seasons.
  - No taste problem.
  - Effective in acute conditions and emergencies.
- Dosage comparison:
  - Powders: 6–12 gm
  - Decoctions: 20–40 ml
  - Asava/Arista: 15–20 ml
  - Mineral drugs: 125–250 mg

### 9.4 Important Rasasastra Texts

#### Rasa Ratnakara / Rasendra Mangala (10th Century AD)

- Siddha Nagarjuna.
- First work on Rasasastra.
- Instruments, copper from Makshika, Jasada from Kharpara, gold preparation.
- Sodhana, Marana, Satwapatana.
- 8 chapters; 4 available.
- 18 Parada Samskaras.
- Mandhana Bhairava Ras, Dasa Moola quath.
- Discussion among Nagarjuna, Salivahana, Mandavya, Vatyakshini, Ratnaghosh.

#### Kakshaputa Tantra (10th Century AD)

- Siddha Nagarjuna.
- 106 queries, 20 Patalas.
- Agni Shambhana, Gati Shambhana, Jala Shambhana, Indrajala.

#### Rasa Hridaya Tantra (12th–13th Century AD)

- Govinda Bhagavatpadacharya.
- 19 Avabodhakas.
- 18 Parada Samskaras, Abhraka Satwapatana, Grasana, Khecharagati.
- Published by Krishna Gopala Ayurveda Bhavan, Ajmir; Motilal Banarsi Das.

#### Rasarnava (12th–13th Century AD)

- Bhairava.
- Conversation between Shiva and Parvathi.
- 14 Patalas.
- Equipment, measurements, colors of metals in fire, Abhraka Satwapatana.

#### Rasendra Chudamani (12th–13th Century AD)

- Somadeva.
- Rasa Puja, Rasa Sala, Yantras, Mushas, Putas.
- Classification: Maharasa, Uparasa, Sadharana Rasa.
- 18 Parada Samskaras.
- Published from Lahore, 1929.

#### Rasaprakasa Sudhakara (13th Century AD)

- Yasodara Bhatt; Junaghad, Gujarat.
- 13 chapters.
- 18 Parada Samskaras, Rasa Karpoora, Jasada from Kharpara.

#### Rasaratnasamucchaya (13th Century AD)

- Rasa Vagbhata, son of Simhagupta.
- 30 chapters; Poorva Khanda 1–11, Uttara Khanda 12–30.
- 27 Rasa Siddhas.
- Classification: Rasa, Uparasa, Sadharana Rasa, Ratna, Loha.
- 125 drugs.
- Hindi translation by Sri Ambica Datta Shastry; Telugu translations.

#### Rasa Rajyalakshmi (14th Century AD)

- Sri Vishnudev Pandit; son of Mahadeva.
- Royal Physician of Bukkaraya, Vijayanagara Empire.
- Quoted Charaka, Susruta, Vrinda, Harita, Vagbhata, Atreya, Nagarjuna, Govinda Bhagavatpada.

#### Rasa Sara (13th Century AD)

- Govindacharya.
- 18 Parada Samskaras; Ahiphena.
- Buddhist, Tibetan region.

#### Rasaratna Pradeepa (15th Century AD)

- Rama Raju.
- 5 Adhikaranas.
- Published by Pandit Takur Dutt Multani, Lahore, 1925.
- Quoted Madanapala Nighantu.

#### Rasa Paddhati (16th–17th Century AD)

- Bindu.
- Commentary by son Mahadev.
- Published by Yadavji Trikamji Acharya, 1925.
- Author from Maharashtra.

#### Rasa Ratnakara (14th Century AD)

- Nityanatha Siddha, son of Parvathi.
- 5 Khandas: Rasa, Rasendra, Rasayana, Vadi, Mantra.
- Contemporary of Vidyaranya Swami.
- Unani drugs quoted.

#### Rasa Tarangini (20th Century AD)

- Kaviraj Sadanand.
- New formulations: Mugdha Rasa, Souraka, Sankha Dravaka.
- Published by Narendra Mitra, 1935.

#### Rasajalanidhi (20th Century AD)

- Bhudeva Mukhopadhyaya.
- Sanskrit and English, 1926–1938, Calcutta.
- 5 divisions.
- Parada Guna, Dosha, Sodhana, Marana, 18 Parada Samskaras.

