## Chapter 10: Vrikshayurveda & Pasu Ayurveda

### 10.1 Overview

- Prehistoric man developed close association with animals and plants.
- Pasu Ayurveda branches: Aswa Ayurveda, Gaja Ayurveda, Go Ayurveda.
- Vrikshayurveda: science of plants, cultivation, pests, soil, time.
- Three categories:
  1. Ayurveda for mankind
  2. Pasu Ayurveda for animals
  3. Vrikshayurveda for plants
- References in Kalpa Sutras, Charaka Samhita Siddhi Sthana 11th chapter, Matsya Purana, Garuda Purana, Koutilya’s Ardhasastra, Ashoka’s rock edicts, Mahabharata.
- Nakula and Sahadeva: specialists in horses and cows.

### 10.2 Aswa Ayurveda: Salihotra

- Salihotra: son of Aswaghosha; born in Shalatur near Kandhahar, Afghanistan; 1st century BC.
- Salihotra Samhita / Haya Ayurveda / Turanga Ayurveda / Aswa Ayurveda.
- Father of Veterinary science.
- 8 divisions, 12,000 Slokas.
- Contents:
  1. Nature, varieties, colors, age, control, royal horses, transactions.
  2. Diseases: Jwara, Soola, Akshi roga, Atisara, Hicca, Swasa, Kamala, Visha Vignana.
  3. Fetus, parturition, reproductive disorders.
  4. Oral cavity, tongue, digestive problems.
  5. Demonology, fractures.
  6. Nava Grahas.
  7. Vasti.
  8. Prognosis, herbs: Triphala, Guggulu, Haritaki, Ardraka, Sarshapa, Laksha.
- Lifespans:
  - Elephant – 120 years
  - Horse – 32 years
  - Cow – 24 years
  - Camel – 25 years
  - Donkey – 25 years
  - Dog – 16 years
- Translated into Farsi, Arabic, Tibetan.
- Other works: Aswa Vaidyaka Sastra by Deepankara; Salihotra by Bhoja; Salihotra Samucchaya by Kalhana; Aswa Vaidyaka by Jaya Datta Suri; Aswa Chikitsa by Nakula.

### 10.3 Gajayurveda: Palakapya

- Palakapya Rishi: son of Sanagayana; expert in domesticating wild elephants; court of Raja Romapada, King of Anga Desa; 1st century BC.
- Hasti Ayurveda published by Anandashram Sanskrit Series, Pune, 1849.
- Gaja Ayurveda published by Saraswati Mahal Library, Tanjore, 1958.
- Hasti Ayurveda: 160 chapters, 4 parts:
  a. Maha Roga Sthana
  b. Kshudra Roga Sthana
  c. Salya Sthana
  d. Uttara Sthana
- More than 180 diseases described.
- Fever names in animals:
  - Horse – Amitapa
  - Cow – Eswara
  - Elephant – Palaka
  - Donkey – Ghoraka
  - Camel – Alasaka
  - Goat – Prlapaka
  - Reptiles – Kashnisha
  - Buffalo – Haridraka
  - Dog – Alarka
  - Fish – Indramada
  - Deer – Mriga
  - Birds – Abhitapa
- Other works: Gaja Lakshana by Brihaspati; Matanga leela by Neelakantha; Gaja Darpan by Hemadri.

### 10.4 Gavayurveda

- No specific treatises available.
- Some descriptions in Sarangadhara Paddhati.
- Sahadeva famous as specialist in Gavayurveda.
- Hansadev, Jain Pandit: animals and birds.
- Someswar’s Manasollasa: treatments for mankind, Gaja, Aswa, Go, birds, animals.

### 10.5 Vrikshayurveda

- “Antah Sangna Bhavantyete Sukha Dukhha Samanvith” – plants have life, experience happiness and misery.
- Science of growing trees; well-established before 6th century AD.
- Vatsayana’s Kamasutra: one of 64 Kalas.
- Garuda Purana: pleasure gardens, pavilions, buildings.
- Koutilya: separate department for gardens and forests.
- Surapala wrote Vrikshayurveda; refers to Varahamihira’s Brihat Samhita (6th century) and Sarangadhara Paddhati (13th century).
- Sarangadhara Paddhati: 236 Slokas on plant science.
- Sri Girija Prapanna Majumdar: Upavana Vinoda.
- Raghava Bhatt: Vrikshayurveda.
- Vavilla Ramaswamy Sastrulu & Sons: Telugu Vrikshayurveda.
- Achanta Lakshmipati: plant science through Ayurveda Siksha Series.

### 10.6 Nakula

- Fourth Pandava; son of Pandu and Madri.
- Special abilities: caring for horses and cows; taming and training horses.
- Wrote Aswa Chikitsa; edited by S. Gopalan; published by Saraswati Mahal Library, Tanjore, 1952.

### 10.7 Sarangadhara

- Sarangadhara Paddhati: chapter Upavana vinoda.
- Topics: soil selection, plant classification, sowing, propagation, planting, protection, garden house, nutrient solutions, plant diseases, botanical marvels, experimental results.

### 10.8 Varahamihira

- 505–587 AD; son of Adityadasa; Avanti (Ujjain); worshipper of Sun.
- Panchasiddhantika uses Saka 427 (A.D. 505); died Saka 509 (587 A.D.).
- One of nine jewels of Vikramaditya.
- Jyotisha: Siddhanta, Samhita, Hora.
- Brihat Samhita: 106 chapters, ~4000 Slokas.
- Subjects: planets, geography, Khadga, Angavidya, architecture, iconography, characteristics of men and animals, omens, cosmetics, Vrikshayurveda, precious stones, praise of women.
- Main works: Panchasiddhantika, Vivahapatala, Brihajjataka, Laghujataka, Yatra, Brihat Samhita.

